---
/*
 * WhatHappens — the honest walkthrough. Trust is the product on this page, so every claim
 * below is read straight out of install.sh (repo root; 156 lines of POSIX sh) and is stated
 * in the order the script actually does it. Line references are to that file:
 *
 * RE-READ AGAINST install.sh BEFORE THE NEXT DEPLOY. The script was rewritten for the
 * CommonSwarm rename in a different lane while this page was being edited, and it was still
 * uncommitted when these numbers were taken. Every reference below was re-checked against it
 * at that moment — the step boundaries survived the rewrite unchanged — but the file grew
 * from 142 lines to 156, which is exactly the kind of number that goes stale silently.
 *
 *   1. node check BEFORE any download, with the searched PATH printed  -> `command -v node` +
 *      the die() message that prints "$PATH" and names version managers (l.26-40)
 *   2. NODE_MAJOR >= 24                                                -> l.42-47
 *   3. two curl calls into mktemp -d, trap rm -rf on EXIT INT TERM     -> l.61-69
 *   4. missing checksum file => stop                                   -> l.68-69
 *   5. shasum or sha256sum; neither => stop; mismatch => stop and
 *      print both hashes. No flag and no env var disables this.        -> l.74-86
 *   6. walks every parent of INSTALL_DIR for a package.json with
 *      "type":"module" and stops if it finds one                       -> l.97-107
 *   7. chmod +x then mv into $INSTALL_DIR (default $HOME/.local/bin)   -> l.108-110
 *   8. reads --version back OUT OF the installed file                  -> l.115-121
 *   9. PATH advice printed, per $SHELL. It prints the line; it does
 *      not edit any file.                                             -> l.126-136
 *  10. ends by pointing at `cswarm accept --link-stdin`                -> l.151
 *
 * ABOUT THAT LAST ONE — THE OLD WARNING HERE IS NOW DEAD, KEPT SO IT IS NOT RE-DERIVED.
 * This note used to read: the script ends on `coswarm accept <invite-link>`, the POSITIONAL
 * form, which our own --help annotates "# unsafe: shell history/process list"; the note
 * recorded what the script printed rather than what we wished it printed, and told the reader
 * to fix the script first. THE SCRIPT IS FIXED. l.151-153 now print `cswarm accept
 * --link-stdin` followed by "Paste the link when prompted -- passing it as an argument would
 * leave a live capability in your shell history." The positional form is gone from the
 * installer entirely. So the script, this page and AfterInstall.astro finally agree, and
 * AfterInstall's matching "still unfixed upstream" note went with it.
 *
 * The name and the identifiers below were checked against the rewritten script, not assumed:
 * it installs `cswarm` (l.65, l.109), verifies `cswarm.sha256` (l.68, l.74), and reads
 * CSWARM_INSTALL_DIR and CSWARM_VERSION (l.13-14). ~/.cswarm is storage.ts:92 and :601.
 *
 * "Two network requests" is the two curl calls at l.65 and l.68 — the only network calls in
 * the file. Do not soften that into "minimal network activity"; the number is checkable and
 * the number is the point. Still two after the rewrite.
 *
 * ~/.cswarm is where the CLI keeps sign-in state, NOT something the installer creates:
 * src/cloud/storage.ts:92 (credentials.d) and :601 (agent-state), created 0700 with 0600
 * files. Stated here because "what is on my machine afterwards" is the question a reader is
 * actually asking. The directory was `~/.coswarm` before the rename; storage.ts now joins
 * homedir() with ".cswarm" at both sites, and carries its own note saying the old directory
 * is deliberately not read. The 0700/0600 line numbers were not re-taken after that file
 * moved and may have drifted; the modes themselves were not re-checked here.
 */

const steps = [
  {
    t: "It checks Node before downloading anything",
    d: "CommonSwarm ships JavaScript, not a native binary, so it needs Node 24 or newer. If node isn’t there, the script prints the PATH it searched and names the likely cause — nvm, fnm and asdf set up node from your shell’s startup files, so it’s invisible to a non-interactive shell. Telling someone already running v24 to install Node would be a false statement on the very first thing they see.",
  },
  {
    t: "It downloads two files into a temp directory",
    d: "The cswarm executable and its published cswarm.sha256. Those are the only two network requests the script makes. The temp directory is deleted when the script exits, whichever way it exits.",
  },
  {
    t: "It computes the checksum itself and compares",
    d: "If the checksum file is missing, if your machine has neither shasum nor sha256sum, or if the two hashes disagree, it stops and installs nothing — and prints both hashes so you can see what it saw. There is no skip flag and no environment variable that turns this off.",
  },
  {
    t: "It looks at where it’s about to write",
    d: "The bundle is CommonJS, and Node picks CJS or ESM from the nearest package.json. So the script walks every parent of the install directory, and if one declares \"type\": \"module\" it stops and explains — rather than leaving you with “require is not defined in ES module scope” after an install that otherwise looked fine.",
  },
  {
    t: "It moves one file into place",
    d: "~/.local/bin/cswarm, marked executable. No sudo. Nothing outside your home directory, no node_modules tree, no background service, and nothing added to your login items. Set CSWARM_INSTALL_DIR to send it elsewhere, or CSWARM_VERSION to pin a release.",
  },
  {
    t: "It reads the version back off disk, then gets out of the way",
    d: "Not the version it meant to install — the one the installed file reports. If ~/.local/bin isn’t on your PATH it prints the exact line to add for your shell, and leaves you to add it: the script never edits a profile file. Then it prints the one command to run next.",
  },
];

const nots = [
  "No sudo, at any point",
  "No shell profile is edited",
  "Two network requests, both to the release",
  "No background service, no login item",
];
---

<section class="wh section section--tight" id="what-happens" aria-labelledby="wh-title">
  <div class="page">
    <div class="wh__head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Transparency</p>
      <h2 class="h-section" id="wh-title">What happens on your machine.</h2>
      <p class="lede wh__lede">
        Piping a script into a shell deserves the suspicion it gets. Here is every step of
        that script, in the order it runs them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="wh__steps reveal-stagger">
      {
        steps.map((s, i) => (
          <li class="wh__step">
            <span class="wh__n mono" aria-hidden="true">
              {String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
            </span>
            <div class="wh__body">
              <h3 class="wh__t">{s.t}</h3>
              <p class="wh__d">{s.d}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <div class="wh__foot">
      <ul class="wh__nots">
        {
          nots.map((n) => (
            <li class="wh__not">
              <svg
                class="wh__tick"
                viewBox="0 0 16 16"
                width="13"
                height="13"
                fill="none"
                aria-hidden="true"
              >
                <path
                  d="M2.5 8.5l3.5 3.5 7.5-8"
                  stroke="currentColor"
                  stroke-width="2.2"
                  stroke-linecap="round"
                  stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
              </svg>
              {n}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="wh__uninstall">
        <strong>Removing it is one file.</strong> Delete
        <span class="mono">~/.local/bin/cswarm</span> and it’s gone. If you get as far as
        signing in, the CLI keeps that in <span class="mono">~/.cswarm</span> — a directory
        it creates at 0700 with 0600 files — so
        <span class="mono">rm -rf ~/.cswarm</span> clears the rest.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* INTEGRATION: this was `.section` (--pad-section, 128px at 1440) with a one-sided
     override pulling the bottom edge back to --pad-section-tight. Measured across all four
     routes, EVERY other top-level section on the site is .section--tight (86.4px) — the
     landing page’s five sections, and /download’s own OtherWays. This page alone read
     128 / 86.4 / 86.4 / 86.4 / 128 down the scroll. It is .section--tight now, so the
     override is gone and the rhythm is one number everywhere. */

  .wh__head {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .wh__head .h-section {
    margin: var(--s-4) 0 0;
  }

  .wh__lede {
    margin: var(--stack-sm) auto 0;
    font-size: var(--t-lg);
    line-height: var(--lh-lg);
  }

  .wh__steps {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--s-10) var(--s-12);
    margin: var(--s-16) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: none;
  }

  .wh__step {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .wh__n {
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--accent-bright);
    padding-top: 2px;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .wh__t {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--t-base);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: var(--track-head);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .wh__d {
    margin: var(--s-2) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    line-height: var(--lh-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .wh__foot {
    margin-top: var(--s-16);
    padding-top: var(--s-8);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-8) var(--s-12);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .wh__nots {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .wh__not {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    line-height: var(--lh-sm);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .wh__tick {
    flex: none;
    color: var(--success);
  }

  .wh__uninstall {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    line-height: var(--lh-base);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 56rem) {
    .wh__steps {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--s-8);
      max-width: 42rem;
      margin-inline: auto;
    }

    .wh__foot {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      max-width: 42rem;
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .wh__steps {
      margin-top: var(--s-10);
    }

    .wh__step {
      gap: var(--s-3);
    }
  }
</style>
